\begin{abstract}
Medical imaging modalities such as ultrasound and X-ray are widely used in clinical practice, where diagnosis follows a structured, evidence-driven workflow aligned with standardized criteria. 
While multimodal large language models (MLLMs) show promise for automated medical report generation, most existing systems rely on end-to-end multimodal fusion without modeling clinically defined intermediate attributes, leading to limited grounding and interpretability.
To address this issue, we propose \textbf{CORAL} (\textbf{CO}ncept-grounded \textbf{R}e\textbf{A}soning with \textbf{L}ocalization), a multimodal framework that integrates spatial grounding and concept-level supervision into a unified reasoning process. 
CORAL employs a prompt-driven medical segmentation model to localize lesions and predicts multi-class clinical attributes through a Concept Bottleneck module. 
The resulting textual concept tokens are combined with mask-modulated visual features within an MLLM to enable structured report generation and diagnostic prediction.
Experiments on BUS-CoT and IU X-ray datasets demonstrate consistent improvements in diagnostic accuracy, concept consistency, and report quality over strong general-purpose and medical MLLMs, indicating that concept-grounded reasoning better aligns generation with clinical decision processes.
\keywords{Concept Learning \and MLLM \and Report Generation.}
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Medical imaging modalities such as ultrasound and X-ray are central to modern clinical diagnosis~\cite{orso2025lung,al2025comparing}. 
Clinical decision-making is inherently evidence-driven and guided by standardized diagnostic criteria~\cite{sackett1996evidence,sackett1997evidence}. 
In practice, physicians first localize suspicious regions and then evaluate structured morphological attributes before establishing a diagnosis.
This coarse-to-fine reasoning paradigm grounds conclusions in explicit visual evidence rather than implicit impressions.
Recent medical multimodal large language models (MLLMs)~\cite{moor2023med,li2023llava,sellergren2025medgemma,xu2025lingshu} have demonstrated promising performance in instruction-driven report generation and visual question answering. 
However, most models rely on implicit multimodal fusion, mapping visual features directly to textual outputs in an end-to-end manner without introducing explicitly supervised intermediate representations aligned with standardized diagnostic attributes. 
Perceptual evidence and diagnostic predictions are jointly optimized within a single latent space, leaving clinical decision criteria implicitly embedded rather than structurally enforced.
To improve visual grounding, subsequent works incorporate region-level or pixel-level supervision~\cite{zhang2023huatuogpt,wang2024interpretable,chen2024sam4mllm,song2025sim4seg,lai2024lisa}. 
By directly generating textual instructions that include bounding boxes or segmentation masks, these methods compel models to perceive where abnormalities occur. 
Nevertheless, this strategy often leads models to overfit the textual formatting of instructions rather than learn \textbf{robust, fine-grained} visual representations.
Abnormalities or pathological findings are frequently small or irregularly shaped, making direct end-to-end generation of segmentation masks or bounding boxes from textual inputs extremely challenging without representation-level supervision. 
More critically, in clinical practice, \textbf{diagnostic decision-making} is complex, involving the integration of multiple standardized attributes beyond lesion localization~\cite{sackett1996evidence,sackett1997evidence}. This reasoning process explicitly transitions from identifying \textit{where} abnormalities are present to evaluating \textit{what} structured characteristics justify the final decision. Existing MLLMs lack \textit{explicit} and \textit{trustworthy} attribute-level modeling and therefore struggle to faithfully approximate the coarse-to-fine, evidence-based diagnostic workflow.
