\noindent \textbf{Comparison with State-of-the-Art Models.}
Table~\ref{tab:report_generation} reports concept consistency, diagnostic performance, and report quality on BUS-CoT and IU X-ray datasets. 
We compare CORAL with representative general-purpose 
(LISA++~\cite{yang2023lisa++}, Qwen2.5-VL~\cite{bai2025qwen25vltechnicalreport}, Qwen3-VL~\cite{bai2025qwen3}, GPT-5~\cite{singh2025openai}, Gemini-3.0~\cite{team2023gemini}) 
and medical-domain MLLMs
(Sim4Seg~\cite{song2025sim4seg}, MedRegA~\cite{wang2024interpretable}, LLaVA-Med~\cite{li2023llava}, MedGemma~\cite{sellergren2025medgemma}, Med-Flamingo~\cite{moor2023med}, Lingshu~\cite{xu2025lingshu}, HuatuoGPT~\cite{zhang2023huatuogpt}).
For baseline MLLMs without an explicit concept extraction module, we inject ground-truth structured concepts into the prompt at evaluation time, thereby removing concept prediction errors and ensuring a fair assessment of reasoning capability.
In terms of concept consistency, CORAL achieves the highest Accuracy and F1 on BUS (0.9023/0.8802), clearly surpassing both general and medical MLLMs (e.g., Qwen3: 0.6581/0.5010), and maintains near-perfect alignment on IU X-ray (0.9823/0.9654), demonstrating robust structured attribute modeling across modalities. 
For diagnostic performance, CORAL attains the best ACC on BUS (0.8017) and IU X-ray (0.9667), exceeding both vision-only and grounding-augmented baselines. 
Regarding report generation quality, CORAL yields the highest BLEU (0.9144) and ROUGE-L (0.9462) on BUS, with consistent gains on IU X-ray. 
These improvements indicate that combining spatial grounding (\textit{where}) with explicit concept supervision (\textit{what}) leads to more clinically aligned and reliable multimodal reasoning.
